The compiler owns a closed set of static cast traits. They provide source
evidence for the plain `cast` expression.

### Trait families

Each of fifteen target families has an infallible and a fallible trait. The
targets comprise all eleven fixed numeric types plus `Bool`, `String`, `Char`
and `Error`:

- `I8`: `CASTABLE_TO_I8` requires `to_i8 |This| -> I8`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I8` requires `try_to_i8 |This| -> I8, Error!`.
- `I16`: `CASTABLE_TO_I16` requires `to_i16 |This| -> I16`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I16` requires `try_to_i16 |This| -> I16, Error!`.
- `I32`: `CASTABLE_TO_I32` requires `to_i32 |This| -> I32`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I32` requires `try_to_i32 |This| -> I32, Error!`.
- `I64`: `CASTABLE_TO_I64` requires `to_i64 |This| -> I64`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I64` requires `try_to_i64 |This| -> I64, Error!`.
- `U8`: `CASTABLE_TO_U8` requires `to_u8 |This| -> U8`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_U8` requires `try_to_u8 |This| -> U8, Error!`.
- `U16`: `CASTABLE_TO_U16` requires `to_u16 |This| -> U16`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_U16` requires `try_to_u16 |This| -> U16, Error!`.
- `U32`: `CASTABLE_TO_U32` requires `to_u32 |This| -> U32`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_U32` requires `try_to_u32 |This| -> U32, Error!`.
- `U64`: `CASTABLE_TO_U64` requires `to_u64 |This| -> U64`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_U64` requires `try_to_u64 |This| -> U64, Error!`.
- `F16`: `CASTABLE_TO_F16` requires `to_f16 |This| -> F16`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_F16` requires `try_to_f16 |This| -> F16, Error!`.
- `F32`: `CASTABLE_TO_F32` requires `to_f32 |This| -> F32`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_F32` requires `try_to_f32 |This| -> F32, Error!`.
- `F64`: `CASTABLE_TO_F64` requires `to_f64 |This| -> F64`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_F64` requires `try_to_f64 |This| -> F64, Error!`.
- `Bool`: `CASTABLE_TO_BOOL` requires `to_bool |This| -> Bool`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_BOOL` requires `try_to_bool |This| -> Bool, Error!`.
- `String`: `CASTABLE_TO_STRING` requires `to_string |This| -> String`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_STRING` requires `try_to_string |This| -> String, Error!`.
- `Char`: `CASTABLE_TO_CHAR` requires `to_char |This| -> Char`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_CHAR` requires `try_to_char |This| -> Char, Error!`.
- `Error`: `CASTABLE_TO_ERROR` requires `to_error |This| -> Error`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_ERROR` requires `try_to_error |This| -> Error, Error!`.

The list gives requirement signatures. A source implementation uses an
ordinary lowercase `this` receiver and its concrete type.

### Contract

- These traits resolve without dependency clauses.
- They cannot be redeclared, imported, exported, aliased or shadowed, and they cannot be used as values.
- The compiler registers builtin conversion evidence, including for `Byte` and the `Dec` family.
- Source-authored traits cover only the fifteen targets above. `Byte` and `Dec` have no source-authored trait families.
- Same-file nominal source types and aligned generic nominal constructors can provide evidence with explicit conformance.
- Matching method shape alone does not prove conformance.
- Infallible and fallible evidence for the same source type and target are mutually exclusive.
- Infallible methods return the target. Fallible methods return the target and builtin `Error!`.
- Evidence follows the complete static source and target types, not known values or optimiser proofs.
- Evidence resolves before HIR. The traits prove source evidence only and add no general conversion framework.

### Infallible evidence

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Label = |
        text String,
    |

    to_string |this Label| -> String:
        return this.text
    ;

    Label must CASTABLE_TO_STRING

    value String = cast Label("docs")
]

The compiler checks that an infallible evidence method does not acquire a
failure contract.

### Fallible evidence

A fallible method returns through builtin `Error!`. Its caller uses the same
plain `cast` syntax:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Label = |
        text String,
    |

    try_to_i32 |this Label| -> I32, Error!:
        value I32 = cast this.text
        return value
    ;

    Label must TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I32

    parsed I32 = cast Label("42") catch |err|:
        then 0
    ;
]

The method's typed error follows ordinary call and catch compatibility rules.
Builtin conversion failures use implicit failure. A custom error still needs
explicit mapping where the destination expects builtin `Error`.

There is no cast-specific propagation marker. Postfix `!` remains the normal
propagation form for a fallible call, not a cast.

### Concepts related to core cast traits

- For cast targets and conversion rules see @../casts (Casts).
- For trait incompatibility see @#trait-incompatibility (Trait incompatibility).
